  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 AM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nMidlevel troughing with its axis cutting through the Ohio Valley \nthis morning will keep a cooler, Canadian air mass influence across \nthe Great Lakes Region. Surface low pressure will move to the \nnortheast this morning and take rainfall with it. No impactful \nweather is expected through the majority of the forecast period \nas surface high pressure begins to build due to upstream 500mb \nridging pattern building across the central U.S. through \nWednesday.\n\nAnother trough currently over the Alberta/British Columbia region \nwill progress toward Hudson Bay this Wednesday. Waves of energy will \npivot across the CWA for the remainder of the forecast period. While \nthe majority of the timeframe is expected to have typical summertime \nweather with sunny skies and highs near normal for early August. \nEnough instability from these waves will keep chances of \nconvection and precipitation in the forecast at times through \nthe remainder of the forecast period. \n\nForecast Details:\n\nHeights begin to rise this morning as upstream subsidence dries out \nprofiles aloft. Some low-level moisture will produce some showers in \na few localized areas this morning, especially northeast northern \nlower, but areas that do observe rain can only expect a general \ntrace to a few hundredths. Expect sky cover to clear today with post-\nfrontal northwest winds to create climatologically cool \ntemperatures. Surface high pressure upstream ridging extending \nthrough the Midwest will slowly build temperatures back to normal on \nTuesday with highs in the low 80s. \n\nThe next midlevel shortwave moves east and develops closed low \npressure over Hudson Bay and begins to pivot waves of energy beyond \nWednesday. Guidance depicts a disheveled frontal boundary to sweep \nacross the CWA, with the highest probabilities of QPF remaining at \nor below a quarter inch. The remainder of the week will look similar \nto Wednesday's outlook with periodic precipitation and convection \nchances at times, but the majority of the week will remain partly \nsunny with highs in the 80s. No strong storms or widespread heavy \nrainfall is expected at this time.",
